 React Native is a popular framework for building mobile apps using React. It allows you to build mobile apps using only JavaScript, while reusing native platform capabilities and components.

Some key features of React Native are:

• Uses the same design as React, so you can reuse your React skills.

• Renders native UI components rather than web views.

• Enables you to access many native platform features like GPS, camera, contacts, etc.

• Cross-platform - you can compile your app to run on iOS or Android.

• Hot reloading - you can instantly see your changes without rebuilding the app.

• Large ecosystem of third-party libraries.

• Integrates with native code when needed.

A basic React Native app looks something like this:

```jsx
import React from 'react';
import { View, Text, StyleSheet } from 'react-native';

const App = () => {
  return (
    <View style={styles.container}>
      <Text>Hello world!</Text>
    </View>
  );
}

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  container: {
    flex: 1, 
    justifyContent: 'center',
    alignItems: 'center'
  }
});

export default App;
```

This renders a centered "Hello world!" text on the screen.

So in summary, React Native allows you to build mobile apps using React and JavaScript, while still being able to access native platform features.

